//? Title: SqueezeNet: Fire Modules, Ceil Pooling and Visible Optimization
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
SqueezeNet reduces expensive spatial-filter inputs with narrow squeeze layers and parallel
expand branches. We check exact Fire equivalence and derivatives, isolate ceil pooling, verify
both released versions and explicitly fit three full-width CIFAR configurations with tensor Adam,
scoring the full CIFAR-10 test set with GPU inference.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.4_efficient_and_mobile_architectures/squeezenet.cpp`.

> The method

A *Fire* module first squeezes Cin channels to S using biased 1×1/ReLU, then expands with
parallel biased 1×1/ReLU and SAME 3×3/ReLU branches of widths E1 and E3. Concatenation
emits E1+E3 channels. Its learned budget, including biases, is

$$P=C_{\rm in}S+S+SE_1+E_1+9SE_3+E_3.$$
\alttext(A narrow squeeze limits channels entering the expensive spatial branch; parallel expand outputs concatenate.)

Fire2 at Cin=96,S=16,E1=E3=64 has 11920 learned scalars versus 110720 for a plain
3×3 to 128 channels. A controlled fixture centre-embeds the 1×1 expand kernel into a 3×3
and concatenates it with the spatial kernel; values and all input/parameter derivatives agree.
That equivalence applies after the nonlinear squeeze, not to an arbitrary dense filter on x.

Full v1.0 uses a VALID 7×7/2 stem to 96 channels; v1.1 uses VALID 3×3/2 to 64.
Both initially pool and retain eight Fire modules. V1.0 later pools after Fire4/8; v1.1 after
Fire3/5. Caffe ceil-rounded 3×3/2 max pooling pads bottom/right with negative infinity when
necessary. At 224 pixels their Fire stage sides are 54/27/13 and 55/27/13; the final map is
13×13×512. Full learned counts are 1248424/1235496. Optional simple equal-width bypasses
add no parameters. The model has no BN or moving-statistic store.

He-uniform initializes Fire/stem filters. After dropout, a biased 1×1 class filter initialized
normal(0,.01) uses ReLU, then global mean. Thus class-map scores are nonnegative; this is not
a generic unconstrained linear head. Large optimizer steps can kill the final ReLU and remove
data gradients. The source port uses Adam(.0001) for that reason. The visible fits use a
warmed-up cosine schedule peaking at .0005: in pilot runs, peaks of .001 and .002 left v1.1 at
exactly chance NLL ln 10, while .0005 trained all three configurations.
No pretrained weights or compression are used; a parameter count is not a compressed file size.

Three explicit fits compare v1.0, v1.0 with simple bypasses, and v1.1. The CIFAR adaptation uses
a SAME 3×3/1 stem at original 32×32 resolution, retaining every Fire module/pool/dropout/class
map. Learned counts are 729034/729034/727626. Each resets parameter, dropout, minibatch and
augmentation seeds. The first training file supplies 8,000 fit and 2,000 validation rows; the
test set is all 10,000 official test images. Each configuration trains on the CPU for ten
batch-32 epochs (2,500 updates) with Adam, a 100-step linear warmup then cosine decay from .0005
to zero, and explicit kernel L2=.0001/2; the three fits together take about twelve minutes.
Independent padded crops/flips precede scaling to [-1,1]. The lowest validation NLL selects
parameters before the official-test evaluation; there is no moving state to save.

All inference (per-epoch validation, the selected training NLL and the test predictions)
runs on the first CUDA GPU when the linked datamunge has CUDA and cuBLAS. The parameter store is
copied with `ParameterStore::to`, and images move in batches of 1,000. Without a usable GPU the
same code runs on the CPU, but scoring 10,000 test images per configuration then takes about
fifty times longer. These are short single-seed fits; they differ from the larger original
source run and ImageNet experiments and do not establish an architecture ranking.

The concept diagram below is drawn by `dm::graphics::Diagram`. Its editable C++
source is the complete block below; regeneration uses the same datamunge toolchain
as the numerical examples. Exports consume the saved local SVG.

